1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,775
Thomas Dunman: The Merchant's
Clerk Who Built Singapore's Police

2
00:00:04,775 --> 00:00:09,133
Dunman Road in Katong and Dunman
High School carry the name

3
00:00:09,213 --> 00:00:13,500
of a police chief who was
almost never seen in a uniform.

4
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:17,255
In 1843, when gang robberies
were a nightly event and

5
00:00:17,335 --> 00:00:21,234
Singapore's police were few,
badly paid and poorly led,

6
00:00:21,314 --> 00:00:26,299
the East India Company's government
handed the force to Thomas Dunman,

7
00:00:26,379 --> 00:00:30,575
a clerk in a merchant firm
with no police training at all.

8
00:00:30,575 --> 00:00:35,264
He ran it for the next 28 years,
and by the time he retired

9
00:00:35,344 --> 00:00:40,275
in 1871 he had turned it into
a disciplined, full-time force.

10
00:00:40,275 --> 00:00:43,825
Singapore's first police force was tiny.

11
00:00:43,825 --> 00:00:47,654
In 1820 William Farquhar,
the first British Resident,

12
00:00:47,734 --> 00:00:52,448
put his son-in-law Francis James
Bernard in charge of twelve men,

13
00:00:52,528 --> 00:00:57,175
among them a writer, a jailer,
a sergeant and eight constables.

14
00:00:57,175 --> 00:01:01,245
In 1821 the merchants
started a Night Watch Fund

15
00:01:01,325 --> 00:01:05,475
to pay for a few extra
men to patrol after dark.

16
00:01:05,475 --> 00:01:10,329
By 1831 the force had only
18 men, and even after it was

17
00:01:10,409 --> 00:01:14,910
enlarged in the early 1840s
to about 130, it was run

18
00:01:14,990 --> 00:01:20,100
part-time by the Sitting
Magistrate, who had other duties.

19
00:01:20,100 --> 00:01:23,700
The town grew much faster than its police.

20
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:28,235
The surveyor John Turnbull
Thomson, who arrived in 1841,

21
00:01:28,315 --> 00:01:32,109
wrote that the real head
of the police was paid

22
00:01:32,189 --> 00:01:36,475
only £60 a year, while the
governor received £5,000.

23
00:01:36,475 --> 00:01:40,457
Gangs of fifty or sixty men
robbed houses near the town,

24
00:01:40,537 --> 00:01:46,050
and Thomson recalled finding the bodies
of murdered men on the public roads.

25
00:01:46,050 --> 00:01:50,310
In February 1843 the government
called a public meeting about

26
00:01:50,390 --> 00:01:54,294
the state of the police, and
the European merchants sent

27
00:01:54,374 --> 00:01:58,500
strong complaints to the
Company's government in Calcutta.

28
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:03,151
Dunman was born in England
in 1814 and came to Singapore

29
00:02:03,231 --> 00:02:07,206
in 1840 as an assistant
in the merchant house of

30
00:02:07,286 --> 00:02:11,937
Martin Dyce & Co. Later in
1843 the government appointed

31
00:02:12,017 --> 00:02:16,325
him Deputy Magistrate and
Superintendent of Police.

32
00:02:16,325 --> 00:02:19,139
The Singapore Free Press
welcomed the choice,

33
00:02:19,219 --> 00:02:23,447
writing that his "intimate acquaintance
with the manners and habits

34
00:02:23,527 --> 00:02:27,000
of the natives" should help
him build a better police.

35
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,800
The appointment was unusual.

36
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:36,007
Senior posts in the Company's territories
normally went to its own "covenanted" officers,

37
00:02:36,087 --> 00:02:39,125
who had signed a bond
of service in London.

38
00:02:39,125 --> 00:02:43,675
Dunman was a "free" Englishman
from outside that service.

39
00:02:43,675 --> 00:02:47,327
Thomson wrote that the government
gave him only the title

40
00:02:47,407 --> 00:02:50,863
of deputy superintendent,
so as not to set a precedent

41
00:02:50,943 --> 00:02:54,269
of an outsider holding the
full post, and paid him a

42
00:02:54,349 --> 00:02:57,950
quarter of what one of its
own men would have received.

43
00:02:57,950 --> 00:02:59,852
Before he joined the police,

44
00:02:59,932 --> 00:03:03,825
Dunman had been well known
in town as an amateur actor.

45
00:03:03,825 --> 00:03:07,130
He performed under the
stage name "Mr. Johnson",

46
00:03:07,210 --> 00:03:10,374
and the historian Charles
Burton Buckley later

47
00:03:10,454 --> 00:03:14,050
called him the best comic
actor Singapore had seen.

48
00:03:14,050 --> 00:03:16,385
Once he was in charge of the police,

49
00:03:16,465 --> 00:03:20,210
the authorities hinted that
the stage and the night watch

50
00:03:20,290 --> 00:03:23,175
did not go together,
and he gave up acting.

51
00:03:23,175 --> 00:03:26,125
Dunman's methods were personal.

52
00:03:26,125 --> 00:03:29,318
According to Buckley,
he spent little time in

53
00:03:29,398 --> 00:03:32,525
his office and was
never seen in a uniform.

54
00:03:32,525 --> 00:03:36,538
He drove around the town and
the country roads at all hours,

55
00:03:36,618 --> 00:03:40,575
sometimes out at four in the
morning and home at midnight.

56
00:03:40,575 --> 00:03:44,395
When a resident complained that
he had seen Dunman driving along

57
00:03:44,475 --> 00:03:48,052
Orchard Road at night without
lights, Dunman admitted it was

58
00:03:48,132 --> 00:03:51,891
not the right thing, but explained
that with lights on he could

59
00:03:51,971 --> 00:03:55,750
not tell whether the police at
the station were keeping watch.

60
00:03:55,750 --> 00:04:00,407
The heads of the Chinese community
and even of the secret societies

61
00:04:00,487 --> 00:04:04,800
trusted him not to give away
where his information came from.

62
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:08,100
He worked on the men
as well as the system.

63
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:13,130
By 1846 he had drawn up the force's
first rules and regulations,

64
00:04:13,210 --> 00:04:18,800
set boundaries for each patrol beat
and introduced a standard uniform.

65
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:22,549
The Indian constables wore
a red turban, a blue frock

66
00:04:22,629 --> 00:04:27,822
coat and white trousers, and the Malay
constables a dark batik headcloth,

67
00:04:27,902 --> 00:04:30,575
a blue jacket and a dark blue sarong.

68
00:04:30,575 --> 00:04:34,426
He ran night classes in the
police stations, because no man

69
00:04:34,506 --> 00:04:38,423
could be promoted to corporal
until he could read and write,

70
00:04:38,503 --> 00:04:42,900
and he pressed for better pay,
shorter hours and a pension scheme.

71
00:04:42,900 --> 00:04:47,986
By 1848 the force had a
Superintendent, a Deputy,

72
00:04:48,066 --> 00:04:52,600
five European constables
and 187 local men.

73
00:04:52,600 --> 00:04:55,300
Some problems were beyond him.

74
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:58,823
In 1846 he told the
Recorder, the colony's judge,

75
00:04:58,903 --> 00:05:02,868
that the illegal gambling
houses could not be put down,

76
00:05:02,948 --> 00:05:07,875
mainly because their keepers had
the money to bribe the constables.

77
00:05:07,875 --> 00:05:11,809
A report by his deputy in
1856 found that the men did

78
00:05:11,889 --> 00:05:15,444
not share a common language,
so that Chinese was

79
00:05:15,524 --> 00:05:19,230
not understood by the
Indian and Malay constables,

80
00:05:19,310 --> 00:05:23,475
and each man kept his record
books in his own language.

81
00:05:23,475 --> 00:05:26,654
Pay stayed low, and many Indian constables

82
00:05:26,734 --> 00:05:29,450
went home after two or three years.

83
00:05:29,450 --> 00:05:32,875
His work also reached beyond policing.

84
00:05:32,875 --> 00:05:36,140
He served on the committee
that ran the fund for the

85
00:05:36,220 --> 00:05:38,778
Pauper Hospital founded by Tan Tock Seng,

86
00:05:38,858 --> 00:05:43,153
and Buckley described him as "a
sturdy beggar for helpless beggars",

87
00:05:43,233 --> 00:05:46,900
collecting money from wealthy
merchants for the patients.

88
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:51,158
After raids on cockfights he
sometimes sent the hospital a

89
00:05:51,238 --> 00:05:56,100
handcart full of the dead birds
to add to its meagre food supply.

90
00:05:56,100 --> 00:06:00,613
The greatest danger to order came
from the Chinese secret societies,

91
00:06:00,693 --> 00:06:04,475
which were rival brotherhoods
with thousands of members.

92
00:06:04,475 --> 00:06:09,226
In March 1846 the head of one
society died, and the magistrates

93
00:06:09,306 --> 00:06:14,364
agreed to a funeral procession of
no more than a hundred followers,

94
00:06:14,444 --> 00:06:18,125
keeping to the direct
road to the burial ground.

95
00:06:18,125 --> 00:06:21,124
Nearly six thousand
people gathered instead

96
00:06:21,204 --> 00:06:23,925
and set off to march through the town.

97
00:06:23,925 --> 00:06:27,368
When Dunman went to reason
with the society's leaders,

98
00:06:27,448 --> 00:06:32,260
the crowd turned on his Chinese
interpreter, and Dunman drove the attackers

99
00:06:32,340 --> 00:06:36,450
off with the butt of a musket
and brought the interpreter away.

100
00:06:36,450 --> 00:06:38,750
Troops had to block the streets to

101
00:06:38,830 --> 00:06:41,350
keep the procession out of the town.

102
00:06:41,350 --> 00:06:45,400
The worst violence came in May 1854.

103
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:48,645
On the 5th, a quarrel over
the weight of a measure

104
00:06:48,725 --> 00:06:52,968
of rice turned into fighting
between Hokkien and Teochew Chinese,

105
00:06:53,048 --> 00:06:55,495
which spread through the town and into

106
00:06:55,575 --> 00:06:58,900
the countryside and lasted
for ten to twelve days.

107
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:01,977
Buckley put the dead at 400 or more,

108
00:07:02,057 --> 00:07:04,600
with about 300 houses burned.

109
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:08,874
Dunman told Governor William
Butterworth that the police could not

110
00:07:08,954 --> 00:07:12,766
stop it alone, but the governor
rode into the town himself,

111
00:07:12,846 --> 00:07:17,925
was pelted by the mob and had to withdraw,
and only then sent for the troops.

112
00:07:17,925 --> 00:07:22,658
About seventy European residents
were sworn in as special constables,

113
00:07:22,738 --> 00:07:25,099
and Dunman, acting as a magistrate,

114
00:07:25,179 --> 00:07:29,354
read the Riot Act before a party
of sepoys entered a house in

115
00:07:29,434 --> 00:07:33,200
Church Street from which the
police had been fired on.

116
00:07:33,200 --> 00:07:36,350
Buckley judged that the
police had been "in good

117
00:07:36,430 --> 00:07:39,848
order and quite equal to
what could be expected from

118
00:07:39,928 --> 00:07:43,225
their small number" against
thousands of rioters.

119
00:07:43,225 --> 00:07:46,332
For his work during the
riots Dunman received the

120
00:07:46,412 --> 00:07:49,648
thanks of the Governor-General
of India in Council,

121
00:07:49,728 --> 00:07:53,175
and Butterworth presented
him with a sword of honour.

122
00:07:53,175 --> 00:07:58,225
Dunman had been made Superintendent
of Police in January 1851,

123
00:07:58,305 --> 00:08:03,600
a promotion the Free Press called
"a very tardy act of justice".

124
00:08:03,600 --> 00:08:06,580
He still had to share
his time with work as a

125
00:08:06,660 --> 00:08:10,400
magistrate and with collecting
the town's property tax.

126
00:08:10,400 --> 00:08:14,963
In 1856 a grand jury criticised
what it called "the present

127
00:08:15,043 --> 00:08:18,111
disgraceful inefficiency" of the police,

128
00:08:18,191 --> 00:08:23,700
and the magistrates voted to give
Dunman sole management of the force.

129
00:08:23,700 --> 00:08:27,256
The Police Act of 1856
then separated the post

130
00:08:27,336 --> 00:08:32,236
of Commissioner of Police from
that of the Resident Councillor,

131
00:08:32,316 --> 00:08:34,925
the senior official in Singapore.

132
00:08:34,925 --> 00:08:39,067
In 1857 Dunman became the
first full-time Commissioner,

133
00:08:39,147 --> 00:08:44,440
on a salary of 1,000 rupees a month,
and the Free Press noted a marked

134
00:08:44,520 --> 00:08:49,125
improvement in the force once
he could give it all his time.

135
00:08:49,125 --> 00:08:52,847
In the years that followed
the police set up stations

136
00:08:52,927 --> 00:08:56,649
along the coast against
piracy, a Marine Police and a

137
00:08:56,729 --> 00:09:00,675
Detective Department to deal
with the secret societies.

138
00:09:00,675 --> 00:09:04,866
In 1865, after clashes between
two rival societies among

139
00:09:04,946 --> 00:09:09,213
the Indian community the year
before, Dunman and Governor

140
00:09:09,293 --> 00:09:12,797
Orfeur Cavenagh banned
the Muharram procession,

141
00:09:12,877 --> 00:09:17,300
and the societies' leaders
were later tried and convicted.

142
00:09:17,300 --> 00:09:21,159
Dunman retired on a pension in 1871,

143
00:09:21,239 --> 00:09:23,975
after 28 years in charge.

144
00:09:23,975 --> 00:09:27,925
Dunman had long had
interests outside the police.

145
00:09:27,925 --> 00:09:31,757
He owned a large coconut
plantation at Tanjong Katong,

146
00:09:31,837 --> 00:09:34,510
which became known as Grove Estate and

147
00:09:34,590 --> 00:09:37,850
stretched along what is
now Mountbatten Road.

148
00:09:37,850 --> 00:09:42,118
He built his own house there and
three bungalows on the shore,

149
00:09:42,198 --> 00:09:45,203
which Buckley described
as the first seaside

150
00:09:45,283 --> 00:09:49,000
houses at Tanjong Katong
for holidays and honeymoons.

151
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:54,559
In town, he had a swampy piece of land
opposite the police station filled in,

152
00:09:54,639 --> 00:09:58,300
and the open space became
known as Dunman's Green.

153
00:09:58,300 --> 00:10:03,664
He was also a founding member of the
Orchard Road Presbyterian Church,

154
00:10:03,744 --> 00:10:05,687
and in 1865 he chaired the

155
00:10:05,767 --> 00:10:09,500
interim committee that
founded the Tanglin Club.

156
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:14,192
After he retired, Dunman spent
four more years in Singapore

157
00:10:14,272 --> 00:10:19,125
looking after his plantation,
then left for England in 1875.

158
00:10:19,125 --> 00:10:24,450
He died at Bournemouth
in October 1887, aged 73.

159
00:10:24,450 --> 00:10:28,397
In 1876 Dunman's Green was
renamed Hong Lim Green after

160
00:10:28,477 --> 00:10:32,570
the merchant Cheang Hong Lim,
who had bought the land and

161
00:10:32,650 --> 00:10:36,750
given it to the government,
and it is now Hong Lim Park.

162
00:10:36,750 --> 00:10:40,125
The force he left behind kept changing.

163
00:10:40,125 --> 00:10:43,054
The Police Force Ordinance of 1871,

164
00:10:43,134 --> 00:10:48,212
which took effect in 1872, was
the first official definition

165
00:10:48,292 --> 00:10:52,075
of police duties in the
Straits Settlements.

166
00:10:52,075 --> 00:10:56,808
In 1877 the government set up
the Chinese Protectorate under

167
00:10:56,888 --> 00:11:02,903
William Pickering to deal with Chinese
immigration and the secret societies,

168
00:11:02,983 --> 00:11:07,074
a commission of inquiry
examined the police in 1879,

169
00:11:07,154 --> 00:11:11,325
and in 1881 the first Sikh
policemen were recruited.

170
00:11:11,325 --> 00:11:14,991
Buckley, writing in 1902,
believed the police had never

171
00:11:15,071 --> 00:11:18,942
been quite the same after
Dunman left, and that a force in

172
00:11:19,022 --> 00:11:22,756
such a mixed town worked best
under someone who knew its

173
00:11:22,836 --> 00:11:26,650
people rather than an officer
brought in from elsewhere.

174
00:11:26,650 --> 00:11:30,400
That idea has returned in a modern form.

175
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:34,057
In 1983 the Singapore Police
Force began setting up

176
00:11:34,137 --> 00:11:37,722
Neighbourhood Police Posts,
adapted from the kōban

177
00:11:37,802 --> 00:11:41,679
police boxes of Japan, so
that officers would be based

178
00:11:41,759 --> 00:11:45,350
in housing estates and
get to know the residents.

179
00:11:45,350 --> 00:11:50,212
By 1993 there were 91 of them across
the island, and in the following

180
00:11:50,292 --> 00:11:54,875
years they were replaced by larger
Neighbourhood Police Centres.

181
00:11:54,875 --> 00:11:58,316
In 2020 the Singapore Police Force marked

182
00:11:58,396 --> 00:12:02,175
200 years since Farquhar's
first twelve men.

183
00:12:02,175 --> 00:12:05,471
Dunman Road runs through
Katong, close to where

184
00:12:05,551 --> 00:12:08,703
his coconut estate once
stood, and the nearby

185
00:12:08,783 --> 00:12:12,375
Dunman Food Centre and
Dunman Lane share the name.

186
00:12:12,375 --> 00:12:17,002
Dunman High School, which
moved to Dunman Road in 1957,

187
00:12:17,082 --> 00:12:21,875
was named after him, and so
was Dunman Secondary School.

188
00:12:21,875 --> 00:12:26,145
A plaque in the Orchard Road
Presbyterian Church remembers

189
00:12:26,225 --> 00:12:30,650
him as a founder member and
benefactor of the congregation.

190
00:12:30,650 --> 00:12:35,483
Why it matters today: Singapore's
reputation as a safe city took a

191
00:12:35,563 --> 00:12:40,700
long time to build, and its first
steps came from an unlikely source.

192
00:12:40,700 --> 00:12:45,108
A merchant's clerk who was hired
because no Company officer was

193
00:12:45,188 --> 00:12:49,668
suitable showed that a small,
poorly paid police force could win

194
00:12:49,748 --> 00:12:54,450
the trust of a mixed and often
divided town by knowing its people.

195
00:12:54,450 --> 00:12:58,076
His name is on a road, a
food centre and two schools,

196
00:12:58,156 --> 00:13:02,700
while the story of how he built
the force is far less well known.
